On October 21, 2026, Railway [disclosed](https://blog.railway.com/p/security-disclosure-wednesday-october-21st) a security incident involving the systems that store customer environment variables. Railway has been open about what happened and has published clear guidance for its customers, and if you're on Railway, following that guidance comes first.

Once that's done, some teams are asking a bigger question: should we stay, keep a fallback somewhere else, or move? This post is for those teams. It explains when Appwrite is a good home for a Railway project and when it isn't, then walks through how the move works, with links to our [Railway migration guide](/docs/advanced/migrations/railway) for every step.

# The short answer

- **Appwrite fits most Railway projects** made of web apps, request-driven APIs, cron jobs, PostgreSQL or MySQL databases, and storage buckets.
- **It doesn't fit everything.** Always-on workers, custom Docker images, Redis, and MongoDB drivers have no direct equivalent on Appwrite. You can move the rest and keep those where they are.
- **The migration uses standard tools.** `pg_dump`, `mysqldump`, `rclone`, and Git move your data and code. There is no lock-in step and no proprietary export format.
- **Don't rush it.** Rotate your credentials first, do a trial run, and cut over in a maintenance window while Railway keeps serving traffic.

# Deciding whether to migrate from Railway

An incident on its own isn't a reason to move. Any platform that builds and runs your code holds your secrets, Appwrite included, and moving providers doesn't make a leaked key safe. The better question is whether Appwrite is a better fit for how your project is built.

## When staying on Railway makes sense

- **Most of your project is long-running containers**, such as queue consumers, bots, or services built from your own `Dockerfile`.
- **You rely on Railway templates** that deploy prebuilt images, such as a self-hosted CMS or workflow tool.
- **Services talk to each other over private networking** on `*.railway.internal`, and you don't want to expose them.
- **Railway works well for you**, and rotating your credentials is all you needed to do.

## When Appwrite is a good fit

- **Your frontend is a framework app.** [Appwrite Sites](/docs/products/sites) builds Next.js, Nuxt, SvelteKit, Astro, Remix, TanStack Start, and Angular from your GitHub repository, with [preview deployments](/docs/products/sites/previews) for every pull request and [instant rollbacks](/docs/products/sites/instant-rollbacks).
- **You want a managed database without running it yourself.** Appwrite's [managed PostgreSQL](/docs/products/databases/postgresql) comes with scheduled backups, point-in-time recovery, [branches](/docs/products/databases/postgresql/branches), read replicas with automatic failover, a connection pooler, and extensions such as pgvector and PostGIS. Managed [MySQL](/docs/products/databases/mysql) is available too.
- **Your APIs and jobs are request-driven or scheduled.** [Appwrite Functions](/docs/products/functions) run per request, on a cron schedule, or on platform events, in Node.js, Python, Go, PHP, Bun, and other [runtimes](/docs/products/functions/runtimes).
- **You'd use more of the backend.** Auth, Storage, Realtime, and Messaging live in the same project, so a migration can also replace code you maintain today. None of them is required to move.

## A partial migration is a real option

You don't have to move everything. A common split is to move the frontend, the database, and the files to Appwrite, and keep a worker or two on a container host. That still leaves you with fewer moving parts, and it lets you move the rest later, or never, once you've seen how the first part runs.

# How a Railway project maps to Appwrite

| Railway | Appwrite | How it moves |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Web app or static site | [Sites](/docs/products/sites) | Connect the same GitHub repository |
| HTTP API service | [Functions](/docs/products/functions) | Replace the server's startup code with a handler |
| Railway Function | Functions on the Bun runtime | Move the file and adapt its handler |
| Cron job | [Function schedule](/docs/products/functions/execute#schedule) | Keep the same cron expression |
| PostgreSQL | [Managed PostgreSQL](/docs/products/databases/postgresql) | `pg_dump` and `pg_restore` |
| MySQL | [Managed MySQL](/docs/products/databases/mysql) | `mysqldump` and `mysql` |
| Storage bucket | [Storage](/docs/products/storage) through the [S3 API](/docs/products/storage/s3) | `rclone copy` |
| Volume | Storage, or a database | Download the files and upload them |
| Variables | [Site, function, and project variables](/docs/partners/project/environment-variables) | Re-create them with new values |
| PR environments | Preview deployments and PostgreSQL branches | Created per pull request |

## What doesn't map to Appwrite

- **Always-on processes.** Functions run once per execution, for up to 15 minutes, so processes that never exit need restructuring into scheduled or event-triggered functions, or a container host.
- **Custom Docker images.** Appwrite builds your code from source on its own runtimes. It doesn't run a `Dockerfile` or a prebuilt image.
- **Long requests and WebSocket servers.** Requests to Sites and function domains time out after at most 30 seconds. For live updates of Appwrite data, [Realtime](/docs/apis/realtime) replaces a custom WebSocket server.
- **Redis.** Appwrite has no managed Redis, so keep it with a managed Redis provider.
- **MongoDB drivers.** [DocumentsDB](/docs/products/databases/documentsdb) stores JSON documents, but MongoDB drivers can't connect to it. You can import your data with [JSON imports](/docs/products/databases/documentsdb/json-imports) and rewrite your queries, or keep MongoDB with a managed provider.
- **Private networking.** Appwrite databases are reached on a public hostname over TLS, with optional [IP allowlists](/docs/products/databases/postgresql/network-security).

# How to migrate from Railway to Appwrite

The [Railway migration guide](/docs/advanced/migrations/railway) covers each step in detail. Here is the shape of the move, in the order that keeps your app online.

## 1. List what you're moving

`railway config pull` writes your project's services, databases, volumes, buckets, and domains to `.railway/railway.ts`, along with the source of each Railway Function. Use it as your checklist, and note each service's repository, build commands, and cron schedule. See [List what you're moving](/docs/advanced/migrations/railway#inventory).

## 2. Re-create your variables with new values

Copy the keys, not the values. Generate a new value for each secret at the provider that issued it, set it as a site, function, or project-wide **global** variable, and mark it as **Secret** so it can't be read back from the Console or the API. Reference variables such as `${{Postgres.DATABASE_URL}}` become plain values, using your new Appwrite connection strings. See [Re-create variables](/docs/advanced/migrations/railway#variables).

## 3. Copy your databases

A PostgreSQL move is a dump and a restore. `railway connect` opens a tunnel over SSH, so the Railway database doesn't need public access:

```bash
# Terminal 1: open a tunnel to the Railway database
railway connect <POSTGRES_SERVICE> --tunnel-only -P 15432

# Terminal 2: copy the database into Appwrite
pg_dump "<RAILWAY_CONNECTION_URL>" --format=custom --no-owner --no-privileges --file=railway.dump
pg_restore --dbname="<APPWRITE_CONNECTION_STRING>" --no-owner --no-privileges --jobs=4 railway.dump
```

Install any extensions your database uses from the Appwrite database's **Settings** > **Extensions** before you restore. MySQL follows the same pattern with `mysqldump`. See [Migrate PostgreSQL](/docs/advanced/migrations/railway#postgresql) and [Migrate MySQL](/docs/advanced/migrations/railway#mysql).

## 4. Copy your files

Railway buckets and Appwrite Storage both speak S3, so `rclone` copies objects across directly:

```bash
rclone copy railway:<RAILWAY_BUCKET> appwrite:<APPWRITE_BUCKET_ID> --progress
```

Files on a Railway volume come off with `railway volume files download` and go into a bucket the same way. See [Migrate storage buckets](/docs/advanced/migrations/railway#buckets) and [Migrate volumes](/docs/advanced/migrations/railway#volumes).

## 5. Deploy your web apps and APIs

Web apps connect the same GitHub repository to Sites, with the same install command, build command, and root directory. Framework API routes and middleware keep working with SSR enabled. See [Deploy web apps to Sites](/docs/advanced/migrations/railway#sites).

APIs keep their business logic and swap the code that starts a server for a [function handler](/docs/products/functions/develop):

```js
export default async ({ req, res }) => {
  if (req.path === '/health') {
    return res.json({ ok: true });
  }
  return res.json({ error: 'Not found' }, 404);
};
```

Cron jobs become functions with the same cron expression. See [Move APIs and cron jobs to Functions](/docs/advanced/migrations/railway#functions).

## 6. Cut over and keep a fallback

Do a trial run first: copy your data, deploy your app, and test it on its Appwrite domain while Railway keeps serving production. Then, in a maintenance window, stop writes on Railway, run the final copy, and point your domains at Appwrite. Keep the Railway project for a few days as a fallback before you shut it down. See [Move your domains](/docs/advanced/migrations/railway#domains).

# Starting your Railway to Appwrite migration

If your Railway project is mostly web apps, APIs, scheduled jobs, databases, and files, it has a home on Appwrite, and you can move it piece by piece with tools you already know. If it's mostly long-running containers, staying put or moving only part of it is a perfectly good answer. Either way, the migration guide shows exactly what each step involves, and if you'd like help planning a larger move, [contact us](/contact-us).

